\section{Factoring the Translation Problem}

In \textit{A First Version of UNCOL}, T.~B. Steel, Jr.\ calls the program that maps a source language into the common language a \emph{generator}, and the program that maps the common language to a particular machine a \emph{translator}. With \(m\) source languages and \(n\) machines, that gives \(m+n\) components instead of \(mn\) direct compilers. This is a count of interfaces to implement, not a bound on implementation effort. If a translator has to recognize idioms peculiar to one generator, or if every generator has to know which machine will eventually run the program, the supposedly factored system still has pairwise dependencies.

Steel's proposal looks like symbolic assembly for an abstract single-address indexed machine, not like a common surface language for programmers. It uses a small set of commands and distinguishes the descriptions of data from the commands that manipulate them. Among other things, the descriptions communicate type, range, and precision so that the translator can decide about storage and instruction selection. Such information must not change meaning across targets: a calculation specified to a certain precision does not become machine-word arithmetic merely because the target supplies a convenient word-sized instruction. Steel also explicitly limits the intended set of language and machine features; universality within a design envelope is a tractable goal, whereas universality without one supplies no criterion for choosing primitives.

\section{P-Code and the Portable Machine}

The Pascal family of P-codes demonstrates a different form of reuse. Rather than starting with many unrelated languages, it starts with transporting an implementation of Pascal. Philip A. Nelson's \textit{A Comparison of PASCAL Intermediate Languages} distinguishes P-4, UCSD, and Los Alamos variants. He describes P-code as assembly for a stack machine, with the interpreter giving an operational account of the machine; translation to another instruction set is also possible. The stack supports evaluation of expressions and holds activation records, while dynamically allocated data requires separate treatment. Procedure calls, frame-relative access, and lexical nesting give the virtual machine a vocabulary particularly suited to Pascal.

Nelson's comparison also explains why a family of portable implementations need not have mutually readable code. The projects adapted the baseline P-code to different goals rather than freezing one contract for all consumers. Thus portability of the \emph{compiler} (the ability to carry it to a new host by supplying a P-machine), portability of \emph{program artifacts}, and interoperability of \emph{versions} are distinct. A stable artifact needs agreement not just on abstract operations but also on their encoding, calling protocol, data representation, and dependencies on the execution environment.

\section{Worked Example: A Stack Contract}

Consider the assignment \(y := a + 1\) in a hypothetical Pascal-like variant with a range-checked destination. The following notation illustrates the virtual contract; it is not a transcription of one historical P-code dialect.

\begin{verbatim}
load.local a             []       -> [Int]
const 1                  [Int]    -> [Int, Int]
add.int                  [Int,Int]-> [Int]
check.range 0, 255       [Int]    -> [Int] or trap
store.local y            [Int]    -> []
\end{verbatim}

Assume addition itself follows the abstract integer type's rules, independently of the destination's range check. On entry and normal exit the operand stack is empty. On a range failure, the store must not take place. In particular \(a=254\) stores \(255\), while \(a=255\) traps for an abstract integer type containing \(256\). Translating the entire sequence into an eight-bit increment followed by a byte store makes the second case silently store zero. The backend can instead widen the computation or use flags and comparisons that detect failure before the update. At every normal program point, the backend must relate live virtual stack slots and frame locations to concrete registers or memory; on the exceptional edge it must reproduce the specified failure. A stack instruction's lack of explicit register operands does not remove either obligation.

\section{Where to Draw the Boundary}

Stanier and Watson's \textit{Intermediate Representations in Imperative Compilers: A Survey} groups representations partly by the organization of control and data. A portable stack format is convenient for decoding and distribution, but it is not obliged to also be the format in which analyses manipulate use--definition links or expose independent computations. A system can decode stack effects into SSA temporaries, optimize there, and preserve the portable execution contract at its public boundary.

When evaluating a UNCOL-like interface, specify which things the producer may assume, which the target may choose, and which services the environment must provide. Integer arithmetic can be fixed semantically while register placement is left open; file access may be delegated to a standard runtime interface. Test new generators and translators against that interface separately, using boundary cases such as the failed store above. This makes the claimed factorization testable rather than just a count of compiler modules.

