\section{Translate Virtual Stack Joins to Explicit Block Parameters}

A stack format can hide value names without making values ambiguous at a join. Consider a hypothetical stack bytecode with an already decoded control-flow graph and verified incoming stack shapes for all reachable blocks. Each instruction has a known sequence of popped types and pushed results; it also has defined operand order. The translator assigns one parameter to each incoming stack slot at every block, including loop headers. Given an empty stack at function entry, the output is a graph in which every transfer supplies explicit names for all live virtual stack values. This is an illustrative lowering, not a claim about a particular P-code interpreter.

\begin{verbatim}
lower(graph, incoming_shapes):
  for block in reachable(graph):
    # Predeclare also for targets of back edges.
    params[block] = fresh_names(incoming_shapes[block])
  for block in reachable(graph):
    stack = copy(params[block])
    for insn in block.instructions_except_transfer:
      values = pop_operands_in_declared_order(stack, insn)
      names = emit_explicit_op(insn, values)
      push_results(stack, names)
    control = lower_transfer_operands(block.transfer, stack)
    # Forward slot zero first, even if it was pushed earliest.
    for target in successors(block):
      require types(stack) == incoming_shapes[target]
      emit_edge(block, target, control, bottom_to_top(stack))
    emit_exit_if_any(block.transfer, control)
\end{verbatim}

Let one arm push \(20\) and then \(3\), and the other push \(10\) and then \(4\). The next block subtracts the top operand from the value underneath. The emitted transfers carry \((20,3)\) and \((10,4)\) to parameters \((a,b)\); its explicit subtraction \texttt{sub(a,b)} returns \(17\) or \(6\). Reversing slot order on both edges would still type-check and yield \(-17\) or \(-6\). A malformed arm that pushes only one value should fail the upstream shape verifier; the defensive edge check also refuses to supply an invented operand.

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-191} maps the virtual stack positions to the join's parameter ports.

